    "q-278319": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278319",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25}=1$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-3$ and $k=5,$ so the center is located at $(-3,5\\,).$",
